00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.250
GEORGE MCMULLEN: Hi, everybody.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.240
CARTER PAGE: And
the two of us, we're

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.180
going to be presenting about
BigQuery federated queries

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:44.270
on Cloud Bigtable and
how iCharts uses it.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.710
So cutting straight
to the chase,

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.930
we just announced this
morning that you can now

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.680
run high-speed SQL queries on
unstructured data in Bigtable.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.840
So you don't need to hook
up your Hive or anything

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:54.570
else like that.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.040
You can just connect BigQuery,
and now you've got SQL.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.587
You can basically turn Bigtable
into another virtual table

00:01:01.587 --> 00:01:03.170
in BigQuery, and do
everything you can

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:07.430
do with BigQuery on top of it.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.100
So the advantages of
using this approach

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.930
is you can query row ranges.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.390
So if you can select row keys.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:19.640
Basically you start in
row keys in Cloud Bigtable

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.880
when you're doing
queries through BigQuery,

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.800
and you can get
very fast results.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.010
As long as you're kind
of ranging that data.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.320
If you do full table
scans over terabytes,

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.004
it's not going to be
as fast as BigQuery is.

00:01:33.004 --> 00:01:35.420
And it's not exactly what this
connector is optimized for,

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.920
but you can do it in batch mode.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.961
I'll talk a little bit
more about the performance

00:01:38.961 --> 00:01:41.717
tradeoffs, why this is,
et cetera as we go along.

00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:43.550
The important thing is
that it supports SQL.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.910
It supports both the legacy
SQL and standard SQL.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.572
There is some
performance optimizations

00:01:48.572 --> 00:01:50.030
that are coming on
the standard SQL

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.950
in the next couple of weeks.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.230
And I'll also talk
about that a little bit.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:57.230
And all the time while
you're running BigQuery

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:00.560
against a live real time
Cloud Bigtable database,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.350
you can still use
the Cloud Bigtable

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.750
interface for the
low latency reads

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.790
and writes that it provides.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.280
And by connecting
Bigtable to BigQuery,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.620
it also connects Bigtable
to the Big Query ecosystem.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.112
So anything that can read
from a BigQuery table

00:02:16.112 --> 00:02:17.570
can now read from
a Bigtable table.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:21.780
Here's the agenda.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.196
Basically I'm going
to run through

00:02:23.196 --> 00:02:24.990
real quick how Big Query works.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.490
And then I'm going to go into
some detail about how Bigtable

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.260
works.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.840
I'm going to talk then
specifically about the BigQuery

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.600
Bigtable connector, and
some of the tradeoffs.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.562
Like how you use it, when you
use it, when not to use it.

00:02:36.562 --> 00:02:38.270
Then we're going to
get in and do a demo.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.290
We're actually going
to set up the connector

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.750
and run BigQuery
against a Bigtable.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.810
And then iCharts is going to
come in and show how they plug

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.860
into this connector as well.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.400
So stepping back a little
bit, the broader roadmap here.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.780
Google Cloud Platform has seven
different storage products

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.400
ranging from a memcache all
the way up to a data warehouse.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.600
And what we're going
to be focusing on today

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.819
are these two, about Cloud
Bigtable and BigQuery.

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:07.360
These two databases
are what we refer

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.119
to as our analytics databases.

00:03:10.119 --> 00:03:12.160
And they have different
strengths and weaknesses.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.340
There's tradeoffs for them.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.220
So what Cloud
Bigtable is designed

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.040
for is for being able
to scale to petabytes

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:24.040
and beyond, and be able to
provide very fast single value

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:24.860
reads and writes.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.910
A spot reads and writes in
single digit milliseconds,

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:29.170
even out on a long tail.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.990
Like 99% and beyond.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:34.420
What BigQuery is specialized
for is the ability

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.380
to take terabytes of data
and do aggregation across it.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.750
And we can do aggregation
analysis and return

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:44.210
results in just a few seconds.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:48.580
The inverse of those is
not actually true, though.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.010
BigQuery cannot do very
fast individual spot reads

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:52.810
and writes.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.110
And while you can do
scans over Cloud Bigtable,

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.950
it's not optimized for it in
the same way that BigQuery is.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.710
And so large scans will
end up taking minutes.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.890
And I'll talk again a
little bit why that is.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:09.430
But the basic gist of it is
that the architecture of the two

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.700
systems are different.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.480
So Cloud Bigtable is row based.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.790
All of your data is basically
stored one row at a time.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:20.635
And so the files that underlie
Bigtable are sets of rows.

00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:22.940
So one file contains
a set of rows.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.770
BigQuery on the other hand, is
actually each file is a column,

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:31.490
or actually each column
is a set of files.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:34.060
So if you have 20 different
columns in BigQuery,

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.940
and you read two different
columns from the same row

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.190
in BigQuery, you're reading
from different files.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.410
And I'll explain
a little bit more

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:44.440
why that affects the
relative performance

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.350
characteristics of the two.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.700
So quick dive into
how BigQuery works.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.300
So fully managed, no
ops, data warehouse.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.880
When you hear data
warehouse, store schema,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.460
those sorts of things, that's
where your BigQuery comes in.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.430
Petabyte scale, it's super,
super parallelized for speed.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:01.990
If you ask a
question of it, we'll

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.940
spin up tons and tons of
workers to analyze your data,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.670
and charge you just for
the second that it's up,

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.640
give you the response
back, and spin them down.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.020
It has the convenience of SQL.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.910
So you don't need
software developers

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.610
to ask simple
questions of your data.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:21.130
And it's durable
and highly available

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:25.600
like with similar
characteristics to Bigtable.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.040
The basic journal architecture
for its first decade--

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.500
I think this was published
in a paper around

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.160
in the late 2000s--

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.430
and basically what
you have is it's sort

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:40.180
of like a on spot MapReduce.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.850
You essentially have
these leafs that

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:45.790
will each be assigned to a
chunk of data in BigQuery.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.346
And they will go
analyze it, they

00:05:47.346 --> 00:05:49.720
will aggregate the results,
send it up to the next layer,

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:52.660
to the level of mixers, which
in turn will aggregate again,

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.300
and send it up to the top mixer.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.390
In 2015 we developed the
Dremel X architecture,

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.460
which simplified and
actually sped it up

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.440
where you really just
have one master talking

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.380
to all the shards to
aggregate things up.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.600
And underneath, these talk
to this columnar storage,

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:13.660
which in 2016, we replaced
the previous columnar storage

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:17.830
with capacitor, which is
faster, especially when dealing

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:23.690
with encryption at rest that
we do for everything at Google.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.257
And as I mentioned here,
the important thing

00:06:26.257 --> 00:06:28.090
of having a columnar
store when you're doing

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.640
large aggregate queries is--

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.730
say you have a table that
has 100 different columns

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:37.780
and you want to be able to
aggregate the values in column

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:43.720
A, based on the values in column
J. BigQuery will basically

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.930
rip through those two columns
and will spread those out

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.480
and aggregate them, and
return results back.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.480
If you did the same thing
on Bigtable however,

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.230
Bigtable would be
reading every single row

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.860
as you go along, just because
of the way it's stored.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.400
And that's by design, in terms
of what they're intended for.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.909
So I'll go into
how Bigtable works.

00:07:01.909 --> 00:07:04.450
There are some slides that were
in some of the other Bigtable

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:05.650
table talks today.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.450
I just want to guess
it's here, like,

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.520
how much I need to go
into deep dive on this?

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.260
How many people have been
to other deep dive Bigtable

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.960
sessions today?

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.910
There's enough people
who haven't, so I'm not

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.500
going to rush through this.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.280
So Cloud Bigtable, it's a
fully managed NoSQL database.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.880
Basically kicked off the NoSQL
revolution in the mid-2000s.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:34.850
It led to a bunch of fun open
source solutions that imitated

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:39.767
or either took direct clones
of the architecture described

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:41.725
in the paper, or were
heavily influenced by it.

00:07:41.725 --> 00:07:46.990
This includes HBase, Cassandra,
Hypertable, the NSA built one--

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:49.340
I think it's called Accumulo.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.490
And it's basically inspired
a whole family of databases

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.380
out there.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.360
The basic important
characteristic about it

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.920
is it's incredibly scalable.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.140
So it can scale petabytes,
hundreds of petabytes,

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:01.530
whatever.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.360
And it's very, very low latency.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.600
So even with large data,
even if you're pounding it

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.752
with a million QPS you get
very tight low latency,

00:08:09.752 --> 00:08:10.460
even at the tail.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.960
Single digit
milliseconds typically.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.960
That supports
sequential row scans.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:21.540
So the row keys are
stored sequentially.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.390
Think of it like
alphabetical order.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.550
And this allows you to
do some clever things

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.390
in terms of optimization.

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:30.260
So for example,
one of the examples

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.840
we'll give them the
demo is the idea

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.980
of stock ticker data over time.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.750
And so if you prefix your
row key with a stock ticker,

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:41.240
you can tell it, hey I want to
read the data for this stock

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.970
ticker during this
window, and it will only

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:45.780
read that data there.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.260
The other thing that's nice
about it is under the covers,

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:50.060
because they're stored
together-- so like,

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:54.020
we're going to look at the
stock ticker GOOG, for Google--

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:57.560
when you start reading the first
entry, the first row for that,

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.120
it's going to go
to the File system,

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:00.740
and their story together
in the same files.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.115
It's already going
to have pulled

00:09:02.115 --> 00:09:05.400
that file into memory into the
block cache of the file system,

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.540
and so it's actually
very fast doing scans.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.690
Not quite as fast as BigQuery
for the reasons outlined,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.909
but still pretty good.

00:09:13.909 --> 00:09:15.950
And then the other thing
is it learns and adjusts

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:17.720
the access patterns.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.450
So as you have diagonal
patterns during the day,

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.730
where you're going hot
or you're going cold,

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:25.940
on different areas of the data
Bigtable will learn about this

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.170
and will rebalance.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:30.930
And I'll show how this works.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.410
So here's basically what
a Bigtable looks like.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:36.755
Conceptually, you could
have millions of columns.

00:09:36.755 --> 00:09:38.750
So it can be very wide columns.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.620
You want to have a single row
to have less than 100 megs.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.800
But apart from that, you
have of no constraint

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.740
on number of columns
you throw into a row.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.380
It's a sparse table,
meaning that you

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.690
could have a billion rows,
and each of the billion rows

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:55.273
could have a different
million columns

00:09:55.273 --> 00:09:57.830
than each of the other rows,
and it doesn't affect the space.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:01.670
You only pay for the row column
intersections that you use.

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:04.190
You'll notice that the
row keys are sequential.

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:07.220
That was the lexicographic
or alphabetical ordering

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:08.760
of the row keys I mentioned.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:11.810
And then at the top, there's
this notion of column families.

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.899
In this case it's
follows and friends.

00:10:13.899 --> 00:10:15.440
And that's the only
schema definition

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:16.481
that happens in Bigtable.

00:10:16.481 --> 00:10:18.320
You'll define a column family.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.057
We may define column families.

00:10:21.057 --> 00:10:23.390
And then the columns themselves
are actually not defined

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:24.950
until insertion time.

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.214
So when you make
a mutation, that's

00:10:27.214 --> 00:10:28.505
when that column first appears.

00:10:28.505 --> 00:10:30.601
And it may only
appear in one row,

00:10:30.601 --> 00:10:31.850
or it may appear in many rows.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:33.400
That's really up to use
the application developer.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.630
So there's a lot of
flexibility, particularly

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:38.601
if you're ingesting things
with unpredictable schemas.

00:10:38.601 --> 00:10:40.100
This is one of the
reasons that it's

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.050
a great fit for
IoT, where you can

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:43.660
get some kind of
surprising properties

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:44.922
from different devices.

00:10:44.922 --> 00:10:46.880
And the last thing I'll
point out in this slide

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.869
is that the database
is three dimensional.

00:10:48.869 --> 00:10:50.660
So you have your row,
you have your column,

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:54.831
and then we'll store whatever
number of versions you want.

00:10:54.831 --> 00:10:56.330
You could store
them forever, or you

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:59.530
could put a TTL or max and
reversions to kind of manage

00:10:59.530 --> 00:11:01.790
your data size.

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:05.210
So this is the Bigtable
architecture in a nutshell.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:08.780
The left is kind of a
deep dive into what's

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:09.930
inside our tablet server.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.940
And I won't go into
the details of that.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:13.430
That would take a while.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:16.310
If you're curious go read
the OCI paper from 2006.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:19.430
A Bigtable that goes into gory
detail in terms of how Bigtable

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.244
works, and how it operates
and scales the way it does.

00:11:22.244 --> 00:11:23.660
The important thing
to notice here

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:25.820
is you've got your
Bigtable client,

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:28.400
and it's talking to a
pool of tablet servers.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.800
And each tablet server will
represent a certain amount

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.780
of data that's
stored in colossus,

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:34.380
which is our file system.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.030
And the important
thing there is the data

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.160
is not actually stored
in the tablet service.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:42.110
So they're
essentially stateless.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.720
And they represent and they
pin the data from colossus.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:46.370
And it's our high
speed networks that

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:50.510
allow this separation to
happen without any performance

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:51.934
degradation.

00:11:51.934 --> 00:11:53.600
Then we have a master
disk coordination.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:54.980
And we have Chubby,
which is kind

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:56.688
of like-- if you ever
heard of ZooKeeper,

00:11:56.688 --> 00:11:59.280
it's kind of the same thing.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.330
So when you connect to
Cloud Bigtable from outside,

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:04.960
you talk to a load
balancer proxy.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.442
And then it will actually send
you to the Bigtable nodes.

00:12:07.442 --> 00:12:09.650
We're not going to talk
about the load balancer proxy

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:10.150
right now.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:13.450
It's kind of abstracted out,
as far as you're concerned.

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:16.250
And we'll talk about
how things work inside.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:18.080
So we talked about
the nodes being

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:19.880
separated from the storage.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:21.800
And this allows you
to do two cool things.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:28.190
So one is that you could easily
move data around essentially.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:29.825
So imagine you have
a situation where

00:12:29.825 --> 00:12:31.700
you have this node on
the left that's getting

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.970
a lot of access patterns.

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:37.040
Maybe it contains data for a
certain region of the country

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.140
that just woke up.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:40.910
And what Bigtable do
is it will recognize

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:43.010
this in a matter of
seconds, and then it

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:46.160
will send a signal to that
node to unload the data.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:48.290
And then it will send a
signal to the other node

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.830
to load the data and
start serving this.

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:54.410
And this happens in a
couple of seconds, sometimes

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.660
hundreds of milliseconds.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.667
It's practically imperceptible.

00:12:58.667 --> 00:13:00.500
Whoever happened to be
right at that moment,

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:02.037
might get a little
bit of latency

00:13:02.037 --> 00:13:04.620
on the very long tail, like four
nines or something like that.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:06.902
But for all intents and
purposes, it's transparent.

00:13:06.902 --> 00:13:08.360
And this is happening
all the time.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:09.920
It's a very aggressive
at rebalancing

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:11.990
to keep things even.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:14.900
The other nice thing is you can
resize too kind of on the fly.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:19.640
So if you need to
throw more nodes on,

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.290
unlike Cassandra
or HBase, you're

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:24.080
not also throwing more disk
with the nodes themselves.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:25.730
You can spin up
and spin down nodes

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:28.340
to increase your throughput,
increase your QPS.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.540
You can do that for a batch.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.248
And then after the
batch is done, spin it

00:13:31.248 --> 00:13:33.880
back down again if you like.

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.310
And because it keeps
the balancing nice

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.950
and even, and because
the nodes actually

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:40.750
don't talk to each
other, you don't

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:42.400
get that kind of
exponential price

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.108
that typically comes
from the distributed

00:13:44.108 --> 00:13:46.120
systems, or offering
distributed systems.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:50.770
And so it goes in a straight
line from 3 nodes to 30 nodes.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.050
By the way, so we kind
of give a guidance

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.120
of 10,000 QPS per node.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.180
And that's kind of what
we show on the console.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:05.980
And that's based off of a load
test of 1 kilobyte payloads,

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.770
mixed read right 50/50.

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:11.172
If you have very large rows
that are like 100 megs,

00:14:11.172 --> 00:14:12.880
you're going to get
much lower than this.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:15.844
If you have much smaller
rows that are 10 bytes,

00:14:15.844 --> 00:14:17.510
you'll get much, much
greater than this.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:19.810
So this is just a rule of thumb.

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:21.650
The most important
thing to do if you're

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:23.358
trying to actually
figure out to scale it

00:14:23.358 --> 00:14:24.970
is run your own load against it.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.850
The important point from
this is that you can trust

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:29.750
Bigtable to scale linearly.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:34.270
So as you keep increasing
up, the number of nodes

00:14:34.270 --> 00:14:37.270
will increase your ear load
throughput proportionally.

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:40.684
And in fact, we had a customer
last year who did a load test,

00:14:40.684 --> 00:14:42.100
just to kind of
prove they could--

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.570
and we want to prove we could--

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:47.800
where he ran 3,500 nodes
and got 35 million QPS.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:50.150
Now the thing that's really
cool about 35 million

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:53.470
QPS is they were actually
persistent and durable QPS.

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:55.734
These were two disks.

00:14:55.734 --> 00:14:58.150
If you had turned off the power
right in the middle of it,

00:14:58.150 --> 00:14:59.650
you wouldn't have
lost a single bite

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:01.230
when it came back up again.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:05.290
So its performance is comparable
to an in-memory database.

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:08.350
So the connector here.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:11.090
So as I mentioned, each
database has its own tradeoffs,

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:13.075
so because BigQuery's
column base,

00:15:13.075 --> 00:15:16.026
it allows you to query
terabytes in seconds,

00:15:16.026 --> 00:15:17.650
and aggregate that
data really quickly.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.066
Cloud Bigtable being row based,
and because of other parts

00:15:20.066 --> 00:15:22.420
of its architecture,
gives you single digit

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:23.600
millisecond latency.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:26.070
So very fast.

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.050
To use both, sometimes people
want both aspects of this,

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:30.899
and that's why we
built this connector,

00:15:30.899 --> 00:15:32.315
you previously had
to dual ingest.

00:15:32.315 --> 00:15:34.300
In terms of building
a system, maybe using

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:37.630
a Pub/Sub with a data flow,
riding into both systems hoping

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.280
to stay kind of in sync.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:42.560
And then sending one
program to one or the other,

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:45.970
depending on what
your use case is.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.880
This Federated connector
that we're releasing today,

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:50.920
basically replaces
the column aisle store

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.740
that's at the bottom of
the BigQuery diagram,

00:15:53.740 --> 00:15:55.960
and just puts Bigtable in there.

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:57.967
So you can basically
tell BigQuery either

00:15:57.967 --> 00:15:59.800
go to your native store,
or you can point it

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:01.960
to the Bigtable APIs.

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:04.690
And it basically treats
Bigtable as an application

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.600
would talking to Bigtable.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:10.090
The benefits of this is you
get SQL on the Cloud Big table,

00:16:10.090 --> 00:16:11.110
and that's pretty cool.

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:14.440
Because if you have a simple
question about your data,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:17.920
previously you would have
to either go instruments,

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:18.832
something like Hive.

00:16:18.832 --> 00:16:21.040
Or you'd have to go, have
a developer write a program

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:22.750
and run a MapReduce
and hopefully

00:16:22.750 --> 00:16:24.820
get that answer to you
in the next few hours

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:25.990
or something like that.

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:27.820
Now you can do ad hoc queries.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:29.230
You can keep them canned.

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:32.800
People who are not developers
can actually can ask questions

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.817
about the data that's in there.

00:16:34.817 --> 00:16:36.400
Once you have this
connector in place,

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:38.830
you can use the same tools
that work against BigQuery.

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:41.150
And that's why we
have iCharts up here.

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:43.420
And they're going to show
off a little bit about what

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:45.670
that allows you to do.

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:47.800
No table partitioning
is required.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:49.300
So if you're familiar
with BigQuery,

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:52.360
BigQuery does full table scans.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:55.030
And if you're doing something
like warehousing all your data

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.790
over time, you'll want
to partition your data

00:16:57.790 --> 00:17:00.850
into days or weeks or something
like that, so that you're not

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.590
reading five years
of data when you're

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:05.650
just curious about two days
or something like that.

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:08.770
Bigtable solves this by
providing row ranges.

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:10.810
And rather than defining
it ahead of time,

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.329
you define your row
range is at query time.

00:17:13.329 --> 00:17:16.300
And it will just select the
data that you requested,

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:17.141
and return that.

00:17:17.141 --> 00:17:19.390
And so as long as you're
keeping the row ranges small,

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:23.410
the query times will be very
fast, one or two seconds.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:27.220
And now you only
have-- for the people

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:29.200
who this is a good
connector for, you only

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:30.730
are storing your
data in one place,

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:32.560
and that simplifies
things a bit too.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:35.819
BigQuery is seeing the same
real time data that Bigtable is.

00:17:35.819 --> 00:17:37.360
The trade off, as
I mentioned before,

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.280
is full table scans
are going to be slower.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:43.540
If you still want to do lots of
ad hoc full table scans all day

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:46.510
long and return in really,
really quick times,

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:48.970
you may still need to
do this dual ingest.

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:50.960
If you're doing smaller
subsets of the day,

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:53.376
you're probably going be just
fine with a connector on top

00:17:53.376 --> 00:17:53.950
of Bigtable.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:55.575
And the other thing
I want to point out

00:17:55.575 --> 00:17:57.550
is that Bigtable
connector is read-only.

00:17:57.550 --> 00:18:00.650
So unlike BigQuery, you
can read out of a BigQuery,

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.190
get some results, read it
into another BigQuery table.

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:04.523
You can't do that with Bigtable.

00:18:04.523 --> 00:18:06.880
However, you can
do everything else

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.090
you could do with a read-only
connector on BigQuery.

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:14.800
You can join a Bigtable read
with data on a BigQuery table.

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:18.450
You could even join two
Bigtable columns together,

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:22.130
or two Bigtable tables together.

00:18:22.130 --> 00:18:27.140
So let's stop talking
and start typing.

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:29.630
Let's see, can we switch
over to the laptop.

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:32.510
So I have an instance here.

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:35.180
It is called GCP demo.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:37.940
And I have it sitting
in US central 1c.

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:41.390
And this is important because
BigQuery resides into regions.

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:43.940
It resides in US and
it resides in Europe.

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:46.790
And because of the amount
of data that it's reading,

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:51.570
you need to co-locate your
Bigtable in the same region.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:54.380
So here we have it in
the zone US central 1c,

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:59.540
which is in the same region
as the BigQuery US region.

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:01.285
So let's take a look
at the data here.

00:19:01.285 --> 00:19:05.480
We're going to use
this tool called CBT.

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:11.570
If you use Bigtable, and
you haven't used CBT before,

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:12.830
then you should grab it.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.440
It's a GCloud component,
and it makes it really easy

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.960
to do simple admin things
for the command line.

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:22.320
It's a lot easier than using the
HBase admin tools or whatever.

00:19:22.320 --> 00:19:25.010
And so here I just
basically said,

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:30.140
read the first row of the
data in this table ticker.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:33.890
And start with the first row
that matches the prefix Goog.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:38.150
So we see actually
the opening day

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:42.380
on the stock market for
Google, on August 19, 2004,

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.020
and it closed at
$50,000 that day.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.315
We've got a high and
a low and a open.

00:19:47.315 --> 00:19:48.830
The volume there is 0.

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:52.760
Some of the data in here is
based on what year it was

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:54.440
or whatever.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:57.530
For both the volume and the
open can sometimes be 0.

00:19:57.530 --> 00:20:00.770
So it's just an artifact
of the data essentially.

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:03.110
But we want to do, if you
wanted to actually start

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:06.020
looking at more of this data,
using this interface quickly

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:08.750
becomes very clumsy and
hard to actually parse.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:11.420
So what we want to do is
actually make a BigQuery table

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:15.710
so we can actually do
something interesting with it.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:16.850
So let's see.

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:24.550
So we're going to create
a new dataset first.

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:25.992
We're going to call it demo.

00:20:25.992 --> 00:20:28.450
And as I mentioned before,
we're going to put it in the US.

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:31.420
So it's co-located
with the Bigtable

00:20:31.420 --> 00:20:35.080
that we have running
and US central 1c.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:36.700
So click OK.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:37.630
And that's great.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:39.040
It's pretty fast.

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:41.710
So then what we have to do
is we have to tell BigQuery

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.360
how to talk to Bigtable.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.360
The trick here is Bigtable
has no notion of typing.

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:47.650
It's all binary data.

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:50.980
Bigtable doesn't know if you're
storing an integer or a string

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:55.540
or a movie or some
ciphertext, or whatever.

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:57.040
You just store bytes in it.

00:20:57.040 --> 00:21:00.320
So we need so BigQuery when it's
reading from certain columns

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:02.680
what the type of that data is.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:06.700
And the way we do
that is this is

00:21:06.700 --> 00:21:09.880
the only slightly complicated
aspect of the connector

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:11.980
as you just define a JSON file.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:16.030
If you get this, everything
else is brain dead simple.

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:20.020
So this JSON file
starts with a format.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:24.040
And you have a reserved term
for the format for Bigtable.

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:25.690
So you put in all caps Bigtable.

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:27.610
And then it will be
looking for a source URI

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:30.010
to define where
that Bigtable is.

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:32.530
So we point to
the Bigtable API--

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:33.270
to Google APIs.

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:35.290
Com/Bigtable.

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:39.270
We point to the project
name, project ID.

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:43.990
We point to the instance ID,
and we point to the table ID.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:45.340
Then we set up our options.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:48.970
So the first option that's here
is actually pretty important.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:51.140
It's read row key as string.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:55.030
So 90% of the time,
probably more,

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:57.300
you want to use strings as
your row keys on Bigtable.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.060
It gives you the most
kind of flexibility,

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.270
particularly if
you're concatenating

00:22:01.270 --> 00:22:03.980
a bunch of different
types of data together.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:08.560
The important thing in this
case is if you give BigQuery

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:12.370
a string row key and you say,
my row keys are all strings,

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:15.070
then BigQuery will know
how to be able to select

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:16.840
between individual ranges.

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:19.900
And that's the key to actually
having a fast connector.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:23.860
So when you're selecting between
to between two particular rows,

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:26.500
and you're reading that
100 rows or 1,000 rows

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:29.410
or 10,000 rows or whatever, not
maybe the whole billion rows

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:31.390
that are in that table.

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:35.740
The second thing, only
read latest, you usually

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:37.030
want this set to true.

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:40.210
Now if you recall, Bigtable is
a three-dimensional database.

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:42.040
So each column or
row intersection

00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:44.580
can have any number of versions.

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:46.370
It's interesting for
historical purposes,

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:47.920
usually you just
want the latest one.

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:50.050
But if you don't, you
can set those defaults,

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:52.480
but you typically
want this to true.

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:54.900
And then you actually define
out your column families.

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:57.880
In the simple example we
were working with today,

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:01.090
I have one column family
just simply called T.

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:03.940
And so we specify the
column family ID there.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:09.830
And then I specify a type here,
which in this case is a string.

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:14.680
And in this case, I'm
saying that all the data

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:16.700
in this column family
happens to be a string.

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:18.520
That doesn't have
to be the case.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:20.706
You could either set
a default, and then

00:23:20.706 --> 00:23:22.330
override in columns
that are different.

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:24.496
Or you could set each column
to be a different type.

00:23:24.496 --> 00:23:27.421
You could say this is a
string, this is an integer.

00:23:27.421 --> 00:23:29.170
But it's important to
set the correct type

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:31.390
so BigQuery knows how to
correctly parse your data,

00:23:31.390 --> 00:23:34.270
or how to turn those bytes
into the correct data you

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:35.124
are looking at.

00:23:35.124 --> 00:23:36.790
And then here is the
individual columns.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:40.030
And you can see these match
up with what we saw before,

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:41.410
looking at the
raw Bigtable data.

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:44.500
Symbol, close, date, et cetera.

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:48.820
So the way we actually
create the new virtual table

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:50.205
is fairly simple.

00:23:50.205 --> 00:23:51.580
We use another
command line tool.

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:54.952
This is BigQuery's GCloud
component called BQ.

00:23:54.952 --> 00:23:56.410
And again, if you
use BigQuery, you

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:58.159
should probably have
this tool, because it

00:23:58.159 --> 00:24:00.220
makes your life a lot easier.

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:03.040
We'll call the MK command
to make a new table.

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:06.811
And we specify the project
ID of the BigQuery.

00:24:06.811 --> 00:24:09.310
They're coincidentally the same
place, but just pointing out

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:12.340
is not the Bigtable project,
this is the BigQuery project.

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:15.010
We specify the external
table definition,

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:17.890
the JSON file that
we just looked at.

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:20.410
And then you say the
new dataset that we

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:22.750
created, demo, and then adopt.

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:25.390
And then the name of
the new BigQuery table.

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:27.640
Note that's not the name
of the Bigtable table

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:30.044
that's specified inside
of JSON file itself.

00:24:30.044 --> 00:24:31.960
We're specifying the
name of the virtual query

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:34.750
that we want to have
queryable inside of BigQuery.

00:24:34.750 --> 00:24:35.970
And we do that.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:38.620
And after a long
time, it's done.

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:43.150
So we go back to BigQuery here.

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:44.530
And we'll refresh.

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:46.740
And generally, it takes
about five seconds.

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:48.400
So pretty fast.

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:52.589
And now we have the
BQ ticker table here.

00:24:52.589 --> 00:24:54.130
Let's take a look
at the schema here.

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:56.840
And you can see
it's basically you

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:00.790
got a schema that it's got
a lot more kind of layers

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:02.135
that are in here.

00:25:02.135 --> 00:25:03.760
But the things you
generally care about

00:25:03.760 --> 00:25:08.170
are kind of like you have
your T.symbol, T.close.

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:12.130
In fact, what we'll do here
is we'll grab the close price

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:14.770
on Halloween.

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:22.240
So T.close.Sell.Value
from BQ underscore ticker

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:26.140
where row key equals Goog.

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:32.110
And it will say
underscore 2016 1031.

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:34.710
I'll run this.

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:41.010
And so we get a closing price
of $785, basically, on that day.

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:42.507
But you know, this
is a BigQuery.

00:25:42.507 --> 00:25:44.715
So you can already do spot
lookups inside of Bigtable

00:25:44.715 --> 00:25:46.810
if that's not very interesting.

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:49.440
So you can do some more
interesting sorts of things.

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:56.450
Let's look at the min and
max for the entire month

00:25:56.450 --> 00:25:58.650
of last October.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.300
So because the
values are strings,

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:02.440
you want to cast them to floats.

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:06.690
Were going to indicate that the
min of the low for the month

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:10.530
is going to be called low, the
max of the high is called high.

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:13.950
And we're going to give
it a row key range.

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:16.370
It's going to be
between this and this.

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:20.000
So we could even do a prefix.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:21.750
You could have basically
a row key prefix,

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.240
and just say it
goes up to 2016 10,

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.780
it will basically
do the same thing.

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:28.590
So we do that and we
find out that the low

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:33.230
for the whole month was
$770, the high was $816.

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:34.980
But usually like when
you're plugging this

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:36.563
