is from between the time
that I purchased the stock

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:24.670
until the present time.

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:26.900
And it's doing some
preaggregation for us

00:37:26.900 --> 00:37:32.240
as well, just to
make things fun.

00:37:32.240 --> 00:37:33.990
If I could copy and paste this.

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:43.680
I can even use
this as a data set.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:54.740
And it just basically create
a user entered query here.

00:37:54.740 --> 00:38:00.540
Give it a name,
portfolio by month.

00:38:03.760 --> 00:38:04.540
Create a data set.

00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:15.750
I'll also show you what it
looks like on BigQuery itself.

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:20.530
Give you an example of
what happens on query.

00:38:20.530 --> 00:38:23.740
Hide options.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:26.430
Wow, that was fast too.

00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:29.282
So here, now I have-- we're
seeing a portfolio, all

00:38:29.282 --> 00:38:31.740
the tickers, when I purchased
the shares, how many shares I

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:34.590
purchased, the purchase
price, the price,

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:36.590
and the month together.

00:38:36.590 --> 00:38:38.760
So price is actually the
price during that month.

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:46.250
And you know what
I'm going to do,

00:38:46.250 --> 00:38:49.280
is I'm actually going to do a
little something extra here.

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:55.030
Because the price of
the shares currently,

00:38:55.030 --> 00:38:57.910
versus the number of shares
that I have, is great.

00:38:57.910 --> 00:38:59.890
But I actually want to
see what the value is

00:38:59.890 --> 00:39:02.590
that is currently
existing in my portfolio,

00:39:02.590 --> 00:39:05.660
or how it's changed over time.

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:07.360
And that's really
straightforward

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:10.350
simple calculation.

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:15.490
I'm just going to multiply
purchased shares to price.

00:39:15.490 --> 00:39:18.890
And certainly, you could have
created a view to do this.

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:21.670
But I think it's more
important to actually do it

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:23.857
directly within
the dataset layer

00:39:23.857 --> 00:39:24.940
within our semantic layer.

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:29.320
Because if this were a
more complex query that

00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:34.150
was put onto a
preaggregated dataset then

00:39:34.150 --> 00:39:37.617
you'll lose the capabilities
of filtering and drilling down

00:39:37.617 --> 00:39:38.200
into the data.

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:41.080
So it's more important to have
that directly within our layer,

00:39:41.080 --> 00:39:45.130
than trying to do it
directly within the data.

00:39:45.130 --> 00:39:52.330
So I'm just going to copy
that, and edit our data set.

00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:54.910
And just going to call it value.

00:39:54.910 --> 00:39:57.500
Value.

00:39:57.500 --> 00:40:00.090
Going to give it a float.

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:01.860
Paste in my thing.

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:02.710
And you know what?

00:40:02.710 --> 00:40:08.370
I'm actually also going to
do a little extra formatting

00:40:08.370 --> 00:40:11.300
just so it looks good.

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:15.910
So now I could create a pivot
table directly on this data.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:23.550
So what I'll do is I'll take
the portfolio name that I have,

00:40:23.550 --> 00:40:25.380
and what it does
is goes directly

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:28.980
into BigQuery automatically,
gets all the portfolio

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:31.710
information out,
and then maybe I

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:38.000
want to see how each portfolio
has evolved over time.

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:44.700
Take that, put that
into the columns.

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:46.820
And you know what?

00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:53.950
I'll also add it to the filter
as my trading started in 2014,

00:40:53.950 --> 00:40:57.100
but maybe I'm only interested
in maybe the past six months.

00:40:57.100 --> 00:41:05.490
So I'll just pick a date
range from end to February

00:41:05.490 --> 00:41:07.869
to October sounds nice.

00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:16.570
And then I'll take the value,
which is the calculation that I

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:19.060
entered in to our
semantic layer,

00:41:19.060 --> 00:41:23.860
and I'll add it
right within there.

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:26.710
So now it's actually going
in, creating that query that

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:29.980
breaks it down by portfolio
name over the trading months,

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:34.960
and multiplying the current
price for each individual month

00:41:34.960 --> 00:41:36.470
by the number of shares.

00:41:36.470 --> 00:41:42.040
So I could see each
individual portfolio and how

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:44.020
they've evolved over time.

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:45.130
But this is great.

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:47.296
But maybe I want to get in
a little bit more detail.

00:41:47.296 --> 00:41:49.630
What about each
individual stock ticker?

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:54.880
So I'll just drag
this into our rows.

00:41:54.880 --> 00:41:57.470
And it will perform the same
kind of query over again,

00:41:57.470 --> 00:42:01.850
but drilling into each
individual stock ticker.

00:42:01.850 --> 00:42:04.820
And now we see
energy, every stock

00:42:04.820 --> 00:42:10.560
ticker, all the individual
amounts, financial.

00:42:10.560 --> 00:42:13.860
And what it's doing here is we
have the individual amounts,

00:42:13.860 --> 00:42:16.900
but we also have the higher
level aggregation based off

00:42:16.900 --> 00:42:20.430
of that hierarchy
that I defined.

00:42:20.430 --> 00:42:21.960
If there was some
other data here,

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:23.700
I could switch
these things around.

00:42:23.700 --> 00:42:26.479
Or what I can also do is I
could take the trading month,

00:42:26.479 --> 00:42:28.770
and maybe I don't want to
see it on the columnar level.

00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:31.980
I want to basically put
that up to the rows,

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:36.450
and show it in kind of a
really more hierarchical level.

00:42:36.450 --> 00:42:42.810
So I could see now for
the month of October 2016,

00:42:42.810 --> 00:42:45.240
which is the first month
that I filtered by,

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:49.484
I'm seeing that the
total value is $30,000.

00:42:49.484 --> 00:42:50.400
I'm not doing too bad.

00:42:58.980 --> 00:43:03.300
I don't know how to double
click on your laptop.

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:08.210
And I can see every
single month over time.

00:43:08.210 --> 00:43:09.200
Cool.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:12.730
But again, what
does this look like?

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:16.380
When I just switch this
over to a line chart,

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:20.420
or maybe even an area chart,
and so we could actually

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:22.356
see how this has come over time.

00:43:22.356 --> 00:43:23.480
So this is past six months.

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:27.320
Maybe I just want to see it
through from the beginning,

00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:29.000
all the way back into 2014.

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:29.690
Back in the day.

00:43:32.343 --> 00:43:34.093
This might be a little
bit easier to read.

00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:42.670
I'm looking at my portfolio,
and I'm doing pretty well.

00:43:42.670 --> 00:43:43.910
Maybe only 6x.

00:43:43.910 --> 00:43:47.530
Not as good as if I just
